<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>

  <!--
    Coverage collection settings for the CRAP (Change Risk Anti-Patterns) score.
    Consumed by scripts/Measure-Crap.ps1.

    🔴 COBERTURA IS THE FORMAT THE CRAP SCORE COMES FROM. This is the opposite of
    the usual advice, and it was MEASURED on this repository (ReportGenerator
    5.5.11 + coverlet 6.0.4), not assumed:

      - coverlet's Cobertura carries per-method `complexity`, and ReportGenerator
        COMPUTES the Crap Score from it. It has done so since 5.2.1 ("Added 'Crap
        Score' metric for Cobertura coverage files" in its own changelog).
      - coverlet's OpenCover carries `cyclomaticComplexity` and `nPathComplexity`
        but NOT the `crapScore` attribute. ReportGenerator's OpenCover parser
        READS that attribute rather than computing anything, because the real
        OpenCover tool emits it. Coverlet does not - so an OpenCover-only run
        produces a Risk Hotspots table with Cyclomatic and NPath columns and NO
        Crap Score column at all: a green run with the one number missing.

    OpenCover is still collected beside it, because it is the only source of the
    NPath complexity metric and costs one extra file per suite. Nothing reads it
    for CRAP.

    scripts/Measure-Crap.ps1 does not trust any of the above. It asserts the
    Cobertura files carry per-method complexity BEFORE rendering, and asserts the
    rendered report really does contain a Crap Score column AFTER - which is the
    check that would have caught this inversion on day one.
  -->

  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura,opencover</Format>

          <!--
            🔴 THE SCOPE OF THE WHOLE EXERCISE, AND IT IS AN ALLOW-LIST.

            CRAP is measured for SlayIdleRepeat.Core and SlayIdleRepeat.Application
            ONLY. Those two are the domain and the use-case layer - the code whose
            risk is worth ranking, and the code `23` keeps free of vendor SDKs so
            that a hotspot in it is always a hotspot in OUR logic. Everything else
            is deliberately out of scope: adapters are thin shells around vendor
            SDKs, and the Godot client's complexity is dominated by generated
            dispatch nobody can refactor.

            This is an INCLUDE, applied at COLLECTION time, and both halves matter:

              - Include, not Exclude, so a new adapter is out of scope the
                day it is created rather than the day somebody remembers to add a
                filter. The scope cannot drift open by omission.
              - At collection time, not in ReportGenerator, so coverlet never
                instruments the other fifteen assemblies at all. Filtering in the
                report would still pay the full instrumentation cost on every run
                and then throw the results away.

            Widening this is a deliberate act. Everything below is defence in depth
            that this allow-list already subsumes - kept, not deleted, precisely so
            that widening this line does not silently switch the rest off too.
          -->
          <Include>[SlayIdleRepeat.Core]*,[SlayIdleRepeat.Application]*</Include>

          <!--
            Subsumed by the allow-list above: a test assembly cannot match
            `[SlayIdleRepeat.Core]` or `[SlayIdleRepeat.Application]`. Kept as the
            standing rule for whatever the Include list grows to.
          -->
          <Exclude>[*.Tests]*</Exclude>

          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>

          <!--
            Also subsumed by the allow-list today, and kept for the same reason.

            `**/*.generated.cs` earned its place before the Include existed: with
            SlayIdleRepeat.Client in scope, Godot.SourceGenerators' dispatch
            switches - InvokeGodotClassMethod, Get/SetGodotClassPropertyValue,
            RestoreGodotObjectData, cyclomatic complexity up to 92, written by
            nobody and refactorable by nobody - took every slot in the ranking.
            Note the `-*Generated*` class filter passed to ReportGenerator does
            NOT catch those: it matches the CLASS name, and they sit on ordinary
            classes like Game.Scenes.Board. Worth knowing before anyone widens
            the Include list back over the client.

            No `**/Migrations/*.cs` entry: there is no EF Core migration anywhere
            in this tree (the Postgres adapter carries none), and a filter that
            matches nothing reads as protection while providing none.
          -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/*.generated.cs,**/*.g.cs,**/*.Designer.cs</ExcludeByFile>

          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>

          <!--
            Auto-properties are complexity 1 and either fully covered or not
            touched. Including them buries the methods that actually carry risk
            under hundreds of trivial rows.
          -->
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>

</RunSettings>
